    "lawCode": "Amd §§188, St Fin L",
    "lawSection": "State Finance Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:S8378                REVISED 06/28/10\n\nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act\nto amend the state finance law, in relation to establishing the New York\nfraud, enforcement and recovery act\n\nPURPOSE:\n\nThe purpose of this act is to conform article thirteen of state\nfinance law with 31 U.S.C. 3729 et seq.\nto ensure\nthat the state\ncontinues to receive additional federal funds under section 6031 of\nthe United States Deficit Reduction Act of 2005, and to empower and\nprotect whistleblowers that file an action related to the theft of\ngovernment funds or property.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Amends section 188 of article 13 of state finance law to\nmodify and conform certain definitions to with recent amendments to\nfederal law.\n\nSections 2 and 3: Amends section 189(1) of article 13 of state finance\nlaw to conform liability provisions for defrauding the government\nwith recent amendments to federal law, and changes other provisions\nrelated to fraud under the tax law.\n\nSection 4: Amends section 190(1) of article 13 of the state finance\nlaw to make a technical correction related to local government\nenforcement actions.\n\nSection 5: Amends section 190(2) (a) and (b) of article 13 of state\nfinance law to conform the seal provision for civil fraud actions\nfiled by relators with that of federal law.\n\nSection 6: Amends section 190(2) (d) of article 13 of state finance\nlaw to apply the \"relation back\" doctrine in accordance with recent\namendments to federal law.\n\nSection 7; Amends section 190(2) (f) of state finance law to list\nrules for whistle-blowers who act on behalf of the state or a. local\ngovernment.\n\nSection 8: Amends section 190(9) of article 13 of state finance law in\naccordance with recent amendments to federal law with respect to\ncivil fraud actions filed by relators, and to clarify definitions\nthat have resulted in confusion or inconsistent law enforcement.\n\nSection 9: Amends section 191 of article 13 of state finance law in\naccordance with recent amendments to federal law, and adopts\nadditional protections for whistle-blowers against retaliation by\nemployers.\n\nSection 10: Amends section 192(1) of article 13 of state finance law\nso that the six-to-ten years statute of limitations for filing a\ncivil fraud action to recover stolen government funds or property is\nchanged to ten years for\nall cases, thus eliminating inconsistent litigation and confusion over\nthe current factoral-based test of when a certain limitations applies\nto a corresponding fraud action.\n\nSection 12: Amendments section 192 of article 13 of state finance law\nto clarify how section 3016 of the CPLR applies to a civil fraud\naction filed under article 13.\n\nSection 13: Sets the effective date for the statute for 14 days after\nenactment, and makes it apply to false claims, records or statements\ncovered under current law.\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nThis bill will ensure that the state receives its current share of\nhealth care recoveries from the federal government, which amount to\napproximately to over $20 million a year if the share was reduced\nbecause of recent changes to federal law.\n\nThe bill will also increase the amount of stolen funds and property\nrecovered by the state and local governments. It will also decrease\nthe amount of state and local government funds and property lost to\nfraud by deterring would-be fraudsters from using false claims,\nrecords or statements to obtain government funds or property.\n\nProvides additional recoveries to local governments that successfully\nprosecute false claims, records or statements.\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE:\n\nThis bill would be effective 14 days after passage.\n\n",
